#version 450
//
// The routed expert projections, expert-major AND row-tiled.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS, and it is not the reason the first tiled GEMM was written.
//
// Widening the prefill tile from 32 to 512 cut expert WEIGHT traffic 13.8x and
// bought nothing — 128, 256 and 512 all measured within 2% of 64 (benchmarks.md
// §20). RADV shader stats ruled out the obvious explanations: 5 to 7 waves per
// SIMD at chunk 32, zero spills, zero scratch. So the limit was neither DRAM
// bandwidth nor occupancy, and it was invariant to tile width — which points at
// the per-unit-work cost of the inner loop.
//
// COUNTING THAT LOOP EXPLAINS IT. With one row per subgroup, a lane handling one
// K-group issues 8 weight loads and then, per token, 8 activation loads plus 2
// group-sums plus 1 scale to feed 8 packed dots. At 32 tokens that is 360 loads
// against 256 dots: roughly three MACs per load, and the activation loads
// dominate. Crucially `qb[ab]` is indexed by (token, K) and NOT by row — so with
// one row per subgroup every one of those loads feeds exactly ONE row's dots.
//
// SO THE REUSE THAT WAS MISSING IS ROWS, NOT TOKENS. Here the token loop sits
// ABOVE a row loop: the activation for a token is loaded once and multiplied
// against ROWS rows' weights. Loads per unit of work fall from 11.25 to 3.25 at
// ROWS=4, ACC_N=16.
//
// THE COST IS REGISTERS, and it is why ACC_N must come down as ROWS goes up. The
// accumulator is ROWS * ACC_N floats, and 64 of them is the cliff this card falls
// off (192 VGPRs, 2 waves per SIMD). ROWS=4 with ACC_N=16 sits exactly at 64, the
// same budget the 1x32 kernel already pays.
//
// ONE SHADER FOR GATE/UP AND FOR DOWN. They differ only in whether the activation
// is indexed by the token or by the (token, slot) PAIR — the down projection
// consumed each expert's own silu(gate)*up, and indexing it by token instead is
// the bug that produces fluent nonsense. That is a push constant, not a second
// file, because everything else about them is identical.


// ROWS=2 with a SIXTEEN-LANE clustered reduction: the middle between a 64-lane
// reduction that costs 19% of lane-ops and a lane-per-row kernel that removed it and
// was 2.15x slower on uncoalesced weight loads. See expert_rt_body.glsl.
#define ROWS 1
#define CLUSTER 16
#define VEC4LOAD 1
#include "expert_rt_body.glsl"
